{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 KickTech HumanBadge \u8212- Technical Instruction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li720 \fi0 Companion document to the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "../README.md"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
README
}}}
. Covers registration flow, verification flow, anti-spoof use-cases, privacy details, and the KTOR ecosystem.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 1. Introduction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 KickTech HumanBadge (HB) is a browser extension that lets you \u8212- as an ordinary social-media user, blogger, or message author \u8212- attach a verifiable proof of authorship to specific posts, messages, and emails you publish. Readers running the extension see a \u55357 ?\u56420 ? badge next to content that matches a published registration. If the content is altered after registration, the badge disappears.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This document describes how it works in practice and how to use it. For the high-level overview, see the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "../README.md"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
README
}}}
.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Important framing \u8212- HB is the lightweight, popular-use tier of KickTech\u8217's origin-registry family.} It is designed for everyday content authenticity between people on social platforms and messengers. HB does {\b not perform identity verification of registrants} \u8212- anyone can install HB and register anything on their own account. For authentication that requires verified-issuer identity (banks, public institutions, critical infrastructure), KickTech provides the full KTOR ecosystem (see \u167 ?12). The two systems coexist without conflict \u8212- a user with both extensions installed will, in certain cases, see two different attestations from two different verification scopes.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 2. The principle: proof of action\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 What HB actually proves\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 HB attests to a {\b deliberate publishing action} at a specific moment by a specific account holder. The chain of evidence is:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 1.\tx360\tab The publisher installs HB and is issued a per-installation cryptographic seed (stored locally, never transmitted).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 2.\tx360\tab Every 10 minutes, HB derives a fresh short token ({\f1 KT-XXXXXX} format) from the seed and the current time window. The token rotates automatically.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 3.\tx360\tab To register a piece of content, the publisher must paste {\b the current token} into the content and publish it on their channel.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 4.\tx360\tab The publisher then opens HB and registers the published content \u8212- this hashes the visible text and writes the hash to the public registry on the Base Sepolia blockchain.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 5.\tx360\tab Readers visiting that content see HB cross-reference the visible KT-token + content hash + publishing-channel context against the on-chain registry. A match produces the \u55357 ?\u56420 ? badge.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Why this is hard for bots and adversaries\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The principle is {\b proof of action}, not \u8220"proof of human\u8221". An attacker would need to do all of the following, in real time, to forge a registration:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b Control the publisher\u8217's account credentials} \u8212- to publish content on the authorized channel.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b Hold a currently-valid KT-token} \u8212- which rotates every 10 minutes; harvested tokens expire fast.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b Be at the keyboard during the action} \u8212- the registration is a UI-driven action triggered from the popup, not a passive API call discoverable from outside.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b Not race the rotation} \u8212- by the time a scraped token reaches an attacker, it may already be stale.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The registration is tied to {\b the publisher\u8217's account on their channel} (e.g., your specific Twitter handle, your LinkedIn profile). An attacker impersonating you on a different account cannot produce a matching registration, because the channel-account itself is part of the implicit context that HB checks.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 What HB is NOT\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is essential to be clear about what HB does {\b not} do:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b NOT \u8220"proof of human\u8221"} \u8212- HB does not verify that the publisher is a biological person.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b NOT a digital identity scheme} \u8212- HB does not establish, verify, or attest to a user\u8217's legal identity.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b NOT a KYC mechanism} \u8212- no personal data, no document verification, no biometrics.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b NOT subject to eIDAS, GDPR Article 9, or other regulated-identity frameworks} \u8212- because no personal-data attestation is performed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b NOT an \u8220"officialness\u8221" certification} \u8212- HB has zero identity verification of registrants. The \u55357 ?\u56420 ? badge confirms that a particular account holder deliberately registered content \u8212- it does NOT confirm that the account holder is who they claim to be in a legal or institutional sense. For institutional-trust certification, see \u167 ?12 (KTOR Premium).\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 HB authenticates {\b a specific publishing act at a specific moment}, performed by whoever held the relevant publishing-channel credentials and the rotating token at that moment.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 3. Registration flow (publisher workflow)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is the workflow for someone publishing content they want to authenticate.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Step-by-step\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li360 \fi-360 1.\tx360\tab {\b Install HB} \u8212- see the Install section in the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "../README.md"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
README
}}}
. HB works identically on Chrome, Edge, Opera, Brave, and other Chromium-based browsers.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li360 \fi-360 2.\tx360\tab {\b Open the popup} \u8212- click the HB icon in the toolbar. The popup shows your current 10-minute token (e.g., {\f1 KT-A4F7B2}) and a countdown to the next rotation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li360 \fi-360 3.\tx360\tab {\b Copy the current token} \u8212- one click on the token field.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li360 \fi-360 4.\tx360\tab {\b Paste the token into your content} \u8212- place the token somewhere visible in the post, message, or email. Common patterns:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li720 \fi-360 \endash \tx360\tab At the end of a post: {\i \u8220"\u8230 ? full text of the post. KT-A4F7B2\u8221"}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li720 \fi-360 \endash \tx360\tab In an email signature for a one-off authenticated message\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li720 \fi-360 \endash \tx360\tab As a hashtag-style suffix on social posts\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li360 \fi-360 5.\tx360\tab {\b Publish on your channel} \u8212- post on X, LinkedIn, Telegram, send the email, etc. The content must be visible (not in a password-protected area, not behind a paywall HB cannot read).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li360 \fi-360 6.\tx360\tab {\b Return to HB and register} \u8212- open the popup and click \u8220"Register\u8221" (or use Harvest for batch registration \u8212- see \u167 ?6). HB will compute the hash of the visible text and submit a registration to the public registry.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li360 \fi-360 7.\tx360\tab {\b Wait for on-chain confirmation} \u8212- during beta, expect 5 to 50 minutes between registration and visible badge across all readers\u8217' extensions (gateway snapshot refresh). The popup shows status as it progresses.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li360 \fi-360 8.\tx360\tab {\b Confirm visibility} \u8212- visit the published content as a logged-out user or use a separate browser to confirm the \u55357 ?\u56420 ? badge appears.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Important notes\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b Token must be current at the moment of publishing} \u8212- copy \u8594 ? paste \u8594 ? publish should happen within the same 10-minute window. If you wait too long, the token will have rotated.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b Hash is computed on visible text} \u8212- formatting, hidden metadata, and tracking parameters do not affect the hash. Two visually-identical posts produce the same hash regardless of underlying markup.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b Edits invalidate} \u8212- if you edit the post after registration, the new hash no longer matches the registered hash, and the badge disappears for new readers. You\u8217'd need to re-register the new version.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Smartphone publishing\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li720 \fi0 {\b HB does not run on smartphone apps} (see \u167 ?7). If you want to register content while on a smartphone, open the relevant platform\u8217's website in a desktop/laptop Chromium browser with HB installed, log in there, and use the web version to register.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 4. Verification flow (reader workflow)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is what happens automatically when a reader running HB visits a page.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 What HB does in the background\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 1.\tx360\tab HB scans visible text on the current page for {\f1 KT-XXXXXX}-format tokens.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 2.\tx360\tab For each token found, HB computes a local hash of the surrounding content.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 3.\tx360\tab HB queries the on-chain registry (via the gateway) to check whether a matching registration exists.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 4.\tx360\tab If a match is found, HB {\b additionally verifies that the page\u8217's publishing context (account, channel) matches the registered publisher}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 5.\tx360\tab HB inserts a badge based on the combined result:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li720 \fi-360 \endash \tx360\tab {\b \u55357 ?\u56420 ? badge} \u8212- content registered, hash matches, and publishing context matches. For person-publication surfaces (X timeline, LinkedIn personal feed / profile, Facebook wall), this includes confirming the author handle against the registration\u8217's keyed-HMAC marker.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li720 \fi-360 \endash \tx360\tab {\b No badge} \u8212- no KT-token on the page, no match in the registry, or hash mismatch (content edited after registration).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li720 \fi-360 \endash \tx360\tab {\b Warning badge} \u8212- hash matches the registration record, but the publishing context cannot be confirmed as the registered publisher. This covers two cases: (a) the content is on a different account or surface from the registered publisher (the unauthorized-copy scenario), or (b) the registered author has exercised the right to erasure of their handle marker, after which the author can no longer be confirmed by anyone.\sa180\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Recognizing an unauthorized copy of a registered post\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 When someone copies a registered post to another account (with or without modification), HB detects this in one of several ways:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b Text modified} \u8212- even minor edits (changed numbers, added phrases, removed disclaimers) produce a different hash. {\b No badge on the copy.} The original (on the correctly-registered publisher\u8217's account) still shows the \u55357 ?\u56420 ? badge.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b Text exact + KT-token exact, but different account} \u8212- the hash matches the registration record, but the publishing context differs from the registered publisher. For person-publication surfaces (X timeline, LinkedIn personal feed / profile, Facebook wall), HB performs a keyed-HMAC author-handle check against the registration \u8212- the impersonator\u8217's handle does not match, and HB shows a {\b warning badge} on the copy. For other surfaces (DMs, group / channel / conversation IDs, organization slugs, provider domains), a structurally simpler identifier-match handles the same role.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b Text exact + malicious image/link overlay} \u8212- the visible text still hashes to the registered value, but the post now contains an additional element (e.g., a phishing-image clicking to a malicious URL). In this case the publishing-context check is what protects the reader: a copy on a different account triggers the warning regardless of any added overlay.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b Author has exercised the right to erasure of their handle marker} (see PRIVACY.md \u167 ?9.2a) \u8212- the server-side key that lets anyone confirm the author HMAC has been permanently deleted, so HB can no longer attest to the author of that registration. If the content text is still byte-identical to what was registered, HB still recognizes the content hash but cannot confirm the author, so it shows a {\b warning badge} rather than the \u55357 ?\u56420 ? badge \u8212- the content is recognized but the page is not an authorized author of it (treated as a possible copy).\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In all four cases, {\b the original \u8212- on the correctly-registered publisher\u8217's account \u8212- continues to show the \u55357 ?\u56420 ? badge normally}, except in the erasure case where the original publisher has chosen to remove their author attestation. The detection otherwise applies to the copy, not the original.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Smartphone reading\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li720 \fi0 {\b HB does not run on smartphone apps.} If you want to verify content while on a smartphone, open the relevant platform\u8217's website in a Chromium-based browser with HB installed. The badge does not appear in mobile native apps.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Edge cases\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b Dynamic content (X, LinkedIn feeds)} \u8212- HB re-scans when the page changes (new posts loaded, scrolling, expand/collapse). Some lazy-loaded content may take a moment to badge after scrolling.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b Gmail special handling} \u8212- HB additionally verifies sender-domain authorization and surfaces a warning banner if email contains suspicious links (relay services hiding destination, links to unregistered domains).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b Edited content} \u8212- if a publisher edits content after registering, readers visiting the updated version see no badge. This is deliberate \u8212- HB attests to the exact text that was registered.\sa180\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 5. The Refresh tip \u8212- important\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li720 \fi0 {\b After installing HB, after a service restart, or any time HB seems to not see content you expect it to verify: reload the open tab and press the popup\u8217's \u8220"\u8634 ? Refresh\u8221" button.}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is the single most useful operational tip for HB:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab HB\u8217's on-chain registry snapshot refreshes periodically. After certain events (extension reload, browser restart, registry resync), the snapshot in HB\u8217's local cache may briefly lag behind the current on-chain state.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab The \u8220"\u8634 ? Refresh\u8221" button in the popup forces a fresh snapshot pull and a full re-scan of the current tab.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab If you\u8217've just registered a piece of content and want to confirm it\u8217's visible \u8212- wait the typical 5-50 min beta latency, then {\b reload the tab + Refresh} to see the result immediately.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is {\b not} a bug \u8212- it\u8217's a deliberate consequence of operating on a public blockchain with snapshot-based caching for performance.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 6. Harvest mode (advanced \u8212- publisher batch tool)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 For publishers who post multiple items in a session (e.g., a content creator publishing a series of posts on a topic), Harvest collects candidate content from the current tab for batch registration.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 How to use\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 1.\tx360\tab Publish your content on the channel (with KT-tokens embedded as in \u167 ?3).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 2.\tx360\tab Open the HB popup \u8594 ? click \u8220"\u9935 ? Harvest\u8221".\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 3.\tx360\tab HB scans the current tab and lists candidate items with visible KT-tokens.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 4.\tx360\tab Select the items you want to register.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 5.\tx360\tab Register the batch \u8212- HB processes each in sequence and reports per-item status.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Harvest is especially useful for:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Multi-tweet threads\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Sequential LinkedIn posts\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Bulk-publishing content creators\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Coordinated multi-item publishing sessions\sa180\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 7. Supported platforms\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Web-only \u8212- important\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li720 \fi0 {\b HB works only on the WEB versions of the platforms listed below \u8212- these apps when accessed through a desktop or laptop browser. HB does NOT work on mobile smartphone apps. This is by technical and security design.}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The golden rule for smartphone users:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li720 \fi0 {\b If you want to verify or register content while using a smartphone, switch to a desktop or laptop, open the relevant platform\u8217's website in a Chromium-based browser (Chrome, Edge, Opera, Brave, etc.) with HB installed, log in there, and use the web version.}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The native mobile apps of these platforms (the X app, the Facebook app, the WhatsApp / Telegram / Messenger phone apps, the Gmail mobile app, etc.) are not covered and will not be covered by HB \u8212- mobile browsers on phones currently have limited or no compatible extension support, and native mobile apps are not Chromium browsers.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Browser support\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 HB is a Chromium browser extension and works identically across all Chromium-based desktop/laptop browsers, including:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Google Chrome\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Microsoft Edge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Opera\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Brave\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Vivaldi\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab \u8230 ?and other Chromium-based browsers\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 You can install HB from the corresponding browser store (Chrome Web Store, Edge Add-ons, Opera Add-ons, etc.) when listed there, or \u8212- if not yet listed for your browser \u8212- as an unpacked extension from the GitHub repository (see Install in the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "../README.md"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
README
}}}
).\par}
