{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 How per-platform anti-spoofing works\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The core text-based verification works on all listed platforms. For {\b person-publication surfaces} \u8212- X timeline posts, LinkedIn personal feed and profile posts, Facebook wall and profile posts \u8212- HB performs a keyed-HMAC check that binds the registration to the post\u8217's author. The author handle is never written to the chain in clear: only a server-keyed HMAC is on-chain, and the verifying handle is sent in the verification request body only (over TLS, not logged, not stored). See PRIVACY.md \u167 ?10.2 for the full mechanism, including how cryptographic erasure of an author marker works.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 For {\b non-person surfaces} (DMs, group / channel / conversation IDs, organization slugs, provider-domain patterns), no author handle is involved; a structurally simpler identifier-match handles the same anti-spoof role.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Additional DOM-structure heuristics that detect subtler kinds of impersonation continue to be added incrementally over the beta period.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 8. Anti-spoof use-cases \u8212- real benefits\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Concrete scenarios where HB provides a useful signal that wasn\u8217't previously available \u8212- framed around {\b ordinary people on social media and messengers}, which is the natural HB use-case. For institutional / high-trust scenarios, see \u167 ?9 and \u167 ?12.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 8.1 Your post copied to an impersonator account with a malicious image\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b The scenario} \u8212- You publish a popular post on X or Facebook (a tutorial, an opinion piece, a recipe). You include a KT-token; you registered it via HB. An attacker copies your text VERBATIM, {\i including the KT-token}, to their lookalike account (or a fresh new account that scrapes your photo as the avatar). The text alone hashes identically to yours. To monetize the impersonation, the attacker drops in an additional element \u8212- for example, a thumbnail image that, when clicked, leads to a phishing site.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Without HB} \u8212- Readers may encounter the impersonator\u8217's post first (it\u8217's a different account, but with a similar handle and your photo). They trust the content because the text is identical to yours, click the image, get phished.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b With HB} \u8212- On your original post (on your correctly-registered account), readers see the \u55357 ?\u56420 ? badge. On the impersonator\u8217's copy, the text-hash matches the registration record, but the publishing context differs. On X timeline, LinkedIn personal, and Facebook wall \u8212- where this use-case actually lives \u8212- HB checks the impersonator\u8217's visible handle against the registration\u8217's keyed-HMAC author marker; it does not match, and HB shows a {\b warning badge} (\u8220"content registered, but publication location does not match the registered publisher\u8221"). The author handle stays off the chain in clear; only an HMAC is on-chain, and the verifying handle is never logged or stored. The reader gets a clear signal that this isn\u8217't the original publisher\u8217's post.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Real benefit} \u8212- Defends against \u8220"verbatim copy + malicious overlay\u8221" \u8212- a phishing pattern that exploits the reader\u8217's habit of recognizing familiar text.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 8.2 Your content reposted with tampered details\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b The scenario} \u8212- You publish a thoughtful post on social with specific details (a recipe, the steps of a tutorial, a how-to article, a personal experience description). Someone copies your text but subtly alters key details \u8212- changes a step in the recipe, swaps a number in your figures, modifies an attribution.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Without HB} \u8212- Followers seeing the altered version don\u8217't realize it differs from your original. The modified version spreads, sometimes faster than your original.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b With HB} \u8212- Your original shows the \u55357 ?\u56420 ? badge. The altered repost shows {\b no badge} \u8212- the modified text produces a different hash. The mismatch is a clear visual signal that this isn\u8217't your original published version.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Real benefit} \u8212- Defends against \u8220"tactical misquoting\u8221" \u8212- a common form of misinformation, harder to detect than outright fabrication.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 8.3 Phishing email defense (Gmail-specific)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b The scenario} \u8212- You receive an email purportedly from a service you use (an online shop, a subscription site, a payment service) with a link to \u8220"update your details\u8221" or \u8220"confirm your account\u8221". The sender address looks legitimate; the formatting is right; there\u8217's a sense of urgency.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Without HB} \u8212- You inspect the sender address, hover over links, may or may not notice the underlying domain is suspicious.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b With HB} \u8212- HB checks: is the sender domain authorized? Are the links in this email going to known-authorized destinations? If suspicious links are detected, HB displays an in-Gmail warning banner. You can then forward the email (unchanged) to {\f1 verify@kicktech.io} for human review, and receive an automated reply indicating whether the destination domain belongs to the sender\u8217's authorized list.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Real benefit} \u8212- Replaces the unreliable hover-and-inspect habit with a deterministic check.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 8.4 Marketplace / classifieds listing fraud\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b The scenario} \u8212- You list an item for sale on a social marketplace (e.g., Facebook Marketplace) with photos and a description. You include a KT-token and register the listing. Someone copies your listing verbatim \u8212- same item, same description, same KT-token \u8212- to their account, changing only the contact details to direct buyers to their phishing/scam channel.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Without HB} \u8212- Buyers seeing the copy can\u8217't easily tell whose listing is the real one. The visible text is identical to yours.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b With HB} \u8212- Your original shows the \u55357 ?\u56420 ? badge. The copy shows a {\b warning badge} (on Facebook wall the keyed-HMAC author check is implemented) or no badge on simpler surfaces, because the publishing context doesn\u8217't match your registered identity. Buyers have a clear signal of which listing is the genuine one.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Real benefit} \u8212- Adds an authentication signal to peer-to-peer commerce, where impersonation is a common fraud vector.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 9. HB vs the full KTOR ecosystem \u8212- what HB does NOT do\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 HB is the lightweight, popular-use tier of KickTech\u8217's origin-registry family. There are deliberate limits to what HB does, and where it ends, the broader KTOR ecosystem (see \u167 ?12) takes over.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 What HB does not do\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b No identity verification of registrants.} HB does not verify who you are when you register. Anyone with the extension installed can register any text on their own account. The \u55357 ?\u56420 ? badge confirms that {\i a particular account holder} deliberately registered content via HB \u8212- it does NOT confirm that the account holder is who they claim to be in a legal, institutional, or organizational sense.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b No image-content registration.} HB registers text hashes only. If a post contains an image important to the content authenticity (a logo, a signed document, a chart), the image is not part of HB\u8217's authentication.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b No signature/footer-block hashing.} HB does not register or check structured signature blocks (e.g., an institutional email footer with letterhead and contact details).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b No issuer authentication for institutional accounts.} HB does not have a process where institutions (banks, government agencies, news organizations) can prove they are who they claim to be.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Implications \u8212- and why we deliberately keep HB this way\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab HB is excellent for {\b ordinary social-media and messenger use}. Your friends, followers, and contacts trust the \u55357 ?\u56420 ? badge on your posts as a signal that the post is genuinely yours, on your account, unedited.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab HB is {\b deliberately NOT suitable as an \u8220"officialness\u8221" certification} for institutional communications. A user who creates a fake account named \u8220"Bank XYZ\u8221" and uses HB on that fake account to register a fake announcement would technically produce a \u55357 ?\u56420 ? badge on their fake post \u8212- because HB has no way to verify they aren\u8217't actually Bank XYZ. {\b This is precisely why HB is not positioned for, and should not be used as, institutional authentication.} Doing so would create a misleading trust signal exploitable for discrediting genuine institutions.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 For institutional, banking, public-administration, news-organization, and critical-infrastructure use, see \u167 ?12 (KTOR Premium and KTOOR), which add the missing layers \u8212- issuer authentication, image-content and signature-block hashing, organizational workflows \u8212- that HB intentionally omits.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Coexistence with the full KTOR ecosystem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 HB and the full KTOR ecosystem coexist without conflict. A user with both HB and a KTOR extension installed will, in certain cases, see {\b two different attestations} on the same content simultaneously \u8212- one from HB (popular-tier text authentication, account-level) and one from KTOR (full institutional authentication, including issuer verification and possibly image/signature blocks). The two attestations cover different verification scopes and complement each other rather than competing.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 10. Privacy in depth\par}
