{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 12. The KTOR ecosystem\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 HumanBadge is one component of the broader {\b KickTech Origin Registry (KTOR)} family \u8212- specifically, its consumer / popular tier. The full family has three components, structured by audience and capability.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Components\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b HumanBadge (HB)} \u8212- this extension. The lightweight, popular-use tier for individual social-media and messenger users. Free during beta. No identity verification of registrants (see \u167 ?9). Text hashing only.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b KTOR Premium} \u8212- the full-featured registry infrastructure for companies, public institutions, and government agencies. Designed for organizational-scale registration workflows. KTOR Premium adds, on top of HB\u8217's capabilities:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li720 \fi-360 \endash \tx360\tab {\b Issuer authentication.} Institutions go through a verified-identity process before their registrations carry the institutional-trust signal \u8212- so a Premium-attested post from \u8220"Bank X\u8221" actually reflects that Bank X has been vetted, not just that someone holds an account named \u8220"Bank X\u8221".\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li720 \fi-360 \endash \tx360\tab {\b Image-content hash registration.} Logos, official imagery, and identifying visual elements are hashed alongside text \u8212- authenticating not just the body of a post but also its visual identity.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li720 \fi-360 \endash \tx360\tab {\b Signature/footer-block hashing.} Institutional letterheads and signature blocks are hashed as separate elements, so even a partial copy that preserves the body but tampers with the signature is detected.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li720 \fi-360 \endash \tx360\tab Per-publisher anti-spoof tuning, editorial and compliance workflows, integration with content-management systems and institutional security policies, service-level agreements for snapshot latency and availability, integration support.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li720 \fi-360 \endash \tx360\tab Covered by contractual arrangements (not consumer self-service).\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b KTOOR \u8212- KickTech Origin Open Registry.} A subset of KTOR offered {\b pro bono in narrow scope} to selected large state institutions, banks, and critical-infrastructure operators \u8212- by KickTech invitation, with bounded scope \u8212- for the purpose of testing the use-case of protecting their clients and citizens from fraud that currently slips through existing state-run reporting systems (such as Polish CERT and the 8080 anti-fraud SMS line). KTOOR participation is governed by a specific agreement covering scope, duration, and reporting.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Image- and signature-block hashing \u8212- a KTOR/KTOOR feature, not HB\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A distinguishing feature of the full KTOR ecosystem (Premium and KTOOR) is the registration of {\b image-content hashes} and {\b signature/footer-block hashes} alongside text. HB intentionally does not include these \u8212- HB is the text-focused popular tier. It is possible that future HB versions may add a simplified form of image-block recognition; this is {\b not} in the current beta roadmap.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Complementing state-run systems\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 KTOR is designed to {\b complement}, not replace, existing state-run reporting and alert systems. Examples for Poland:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b CERT Polska} \u8212- the national CERT for cyber threats.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b 8080 (anti-fraud SMS line)} \u8212- citizens forward suspicious SMS to 8080 for analysis.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 These systems excel at threat reporting and post-incident response. KTOR adds the {\b inverse} signal: a publisher-anchored, on-chain authentication that lets citizens see {\i \u8220"this is the genuine version\u8221"} rather than only {\i \u8220"this is suspicious\u8221"}. The intent is to slot into existing fraud-prevention workflows, not to compete with them.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Coexistence with HB\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 HB and the full KTOR ecosystem run side by side. A user with both extensions installed will, in certain cases, see {\b two different attestations} on the same content \u8212- one from HB (popular-tier, text, account-level) and one from KTOR (full institutional, including issuer verification and image/signature blocks). The attestations cover different verification scopes and complement each other.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Inquiries\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b Institutional partnerships, KTOR Premium services, KTOOR pro-bono inquiries} \u8212- {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.kicktech.io"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
www.kicktech.io
}}}
.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b Consumer questions} \u8212- see beta feedback channels in \u167 ?11.\sa180\par}
